  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM MDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nWV imagery shows dry slotting conditions for much of the\nnorthern Rockies with the main finger of the PFJ well north into\nCanada. IR shows mainly clear skies for the state with high\npressure building in from the south behind a weak shortwave that\nhas pushed through much of the CWA east of the Divide. As such,\na weak cold front has pushed through the state affecting mainly\nportions east of the Divide through early Monday morning. This\nwill result in more of northerly direction for winds and\ntemperatures up to 15 to 20 degrees cooler in the afternoon than\nthat of Sunday. Areas west of the Divide, especially south and\nsouthwestern portions of the CWA will not see much of the\naffects. Westerly winds persist in these areas where gusts up to\n30 to 35 mph are likely through sunset before easing into the\novernight hours Monday into Tuesday morning. This will continue\nto the red flag conditions before weaker winds expected Tuesday\nonward through the work week. \n\nGoing forward, expect mainly dry conditions and a slight \nwarming trend through the rest of the week and into the weekend.\nThe only exception will be a shortwave that will skirt by to \nthe northeast, that will have weak PoPs around 15 to 20 percent\nfor the northern Bighorns and into Johnson County Tuesday \nevening through about midnight. Quick moving and progressive, \nthis will have limited impacts and QPF of note. Temperatures \nwill push back to well above average once again for Thursday and\nFriday with areas east of the Divide pushing triple digits once\nagain east of the Divide possibly pushing some records as well.\nNothing of note on the horizon outside of the increasing \ntemperatures and elevated to near critical fire weather \nconditions, but no strong wind events look to occur as well. \nLonger term model solutions show a possible pattern change by \nearly to mid next week as the upper level flow and upper high to\nthe south weaken as trough digs down across the Pacific \nnorthwest. Time will tell, but in the meantime, enjoy the \n\"cooler\" Monday before temperatures push right back up \nthroughout the week with no precipitation of note on the \nhorizon.",
